1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,400
In your name we pray, amen.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:57,340
Well, good morning.

3
00:00:57,340 --> 00:00:58,600
Good morning.

4
00:00:58,600 --> 00:00:59,860
How's everybody doing?

5
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:00,360
How's everybody doing this morning?

6
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:02,120
Good.

7
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:03,520
Some people are good.

8
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:04,880
That's good.

9
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:05,760
I'm glad to hear that.

10
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:10,900
Hey, this morning we are launching a brand new series, as you can see, on the book of

11
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:11,440
Ruth.

12
00:01:11,440 --> 00:01:14,840
I know all of you are very familiar with the story, right?

13
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:16,740
Because we all went to Sunday school growing up.

14
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:18,000
We know all the stories, right?

15
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:19,440
It's okay.

16
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:21,540
It's okay if you don't know all the stories.

17
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:22,520
That's totally fine.

18
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:24,360
That's what today is all about.

19
00:01:24,360 --> 00:01:26,200
We're going to get to know the story a little bit.

20
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,420
I've been personally working through the book of Ruth over the past few months, and it is

21
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:34,140
slowly but surely becoming one of my favorite Old Testament books.

22
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:37,360
And so I'm really excited for us to work through it together.

23
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:43,260
And what I've noticed as I've been studying the book of Ruth is that there's many popular

24
00:01:43,260 --> 00:01:47,560
level studies out there, books and sermons on the book of Ruth as of late.

25
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:51,040
And they've kind of boiled the book down, and maybe some of you that did grow up in church

26
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:55,580
have heard this version of the book of Ruth, but they've kind of boiled it down to like

27
00:01:55,580 --> 00:02:00,520
this nice little love story that we kind of throw into the middle of like the action-packed

28
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:04,860
violence and genealogies of the Old Testament to give the readers a break, right?

29
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:07,900
Like, oh, like, take a breath.

30
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:09,300
Four chapters, love story.

31
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:10,120
This should be nice.

32
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:14,720
I actually heard one pastor call it the rom-com of the Old Testament.

33
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:18,160
Slightly disrespectful, in my opinion.

34
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:22,100
And I understand what these Bible teachers are attempting to do.

35
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:26,860
I don't hold anything against them or even think that some of the implications that they

36
00:02:26,860 --> 00:02:29,020
are drawing out are wrong because they're not.

37
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:34,920
But I believe that underneath the surface of the story, there's far more depth and nuance

38
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:37,740
and biblical significance in the book of Ruth than we might realize.

39
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:43,840
And if we are going to boil it down to a love story, we should at least compare it to the

40
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:50,580
likes of Pride and Prejudice, which is undebatably an incredible love story with depth and nuance

41
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:51,160
and meaning.

42
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:56,900
And if you don't agree with me, find another church.

43
00:02:56,900 --> 00:02:58,640
No, I'm kidding.

44
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:00,120
I'm kidding.

45
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:02,920
But seriously, who doesn't like Pride and Prejudice?

46
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:08,100
And the story of Ruth is perfect for the season of Lent.

47
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:10,640
If you don't know, we're in the second Sunday of Lent.

48
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:16,280
And I won't make you all raise your hands if you have already failed in your Lenten fasting

49
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:18,420
because I don't want to embarrass myself.

50
00:03:18,420 --> 00:03:20,940
Come on, guys.

51
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:23,260
I wrote that joke this morning while we were doing worship.

52
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:24,220
I thought it was going to be good.

53
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:24,560
All right.

54
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:24,880
Anyway.

55
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:28,200
No, it's a great book for the season of Lent.

56
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,480
And we actually intentionally put it in the season of Lent for this reason,

57
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:34,340
that we are invited into the desert.

58
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:37,120
Of life with Ruth and Naomi in the book of Ruth.

59
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:44,980
We get to walk with them through the pits of despair and see God at work in a process of redemption in their lives.

60
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:49,500
It's a book about grief and lament, hope and restoration.

61
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,960
And it invites us to wrestle with God through the painful seasons of our own lot.

62
00:03:54,960 --> 00:04:00,800
It helps us to see how God might invite us to participate in his process of redemption moving forward.

63
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:05,120
So, let's pray and then we'll just see how far we get.

64
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:05,760
Sound good?

65
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:07,260
All right.

66
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:15,680
Father in heaven, we thank you for this time that we have focused our attention on the throne room of heaven,

67
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:23,420
reminded ourselves of your holiness, reminded ourselves, God, that you are totally set apart,

68
00:04:24,080 --> 00:04:26,800
seated on the throne, seated on the throne, high and lifted up.

69
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:33,420
That you are worthy of our praise and attention, our questions, our doubts, our fears, our anxiety.

70
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:34,520
You are worthy of it all.

71
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:36,220
And you're not afraid of it.

72
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:41,120
God, you welcome our wrestling and our struggles and our fear.

73
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:53,520
And the answers that you do give us are sufficient to help us persevere in this life of chaos and sorrow.

74
00:04:53,520 --> 00:05:00,420
And God, when you lovingly and wisely choose not to give us answers because we wouldn't understand them,

75
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:03,380
you comfort us in the midst of our questions and our doubt.

76
00:05:03,380 --> 00:05:04,800
That's really what Ruth is all about.

77
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:09,200
So help us to see, help us to see you on every page of this book.

78
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:17,680
Help us to see how you are interacting with the chaos of our own lives as we see you interact with the chaos of Ruth and Naomi.

79
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:21,720
God, draw near to us and comfort us.

80
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:26,320
And if we're not suffering, give us encouragement to come alongside those who are.

81
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:33,560
To help them endure and persevere and cling to you by faith.

82
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:37,760
I pray all of this in the name of Jesus.

83
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:39,320
Amen.

84
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:46,900
So date nights look a little bit different for Marissa and I now that we have almost two-year-old.

85
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:50,540
First, they're less frequent than they used to be.

86
00:05:50,540 --> 00:05:55,240
And second, we've had to find some creative ways to go about doing them.

87
00:05:55,240 --> 00:06:02,560
And one of our favorite new creative ways to have a date night is that after Rory goes to bed, we bust out these murder mystery board games.

88
00:06:02,560 --> 00:06:05,920
Have any of you seen or played with the murder mystery board games?

89
00:06:05,920 --> 00:06:06,480
A couple of you?

90
00:06:06,480 --> 00:06:06,880
All right.

91
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:07,240
Yeah.

92
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:11,240
They are, in my opinion, a tremendous amount of fun.

93
00:06:11,240 --> 00:06:13,880
They're more fun for Marissa because she's better at it than I am.

94
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:18,320
She grew up on Agatha Christie and I grew up on the Left Behind series.

95
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:24,840
So she's a bit better at looking for the nuanced clues and details.

96
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:28,560
But the way it works is you get thrust into the middle of a crime scene.

97
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:31,720
You're introduced to some characters and to the plot.

98
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:35,120
And you're just kind of sifting through all these different clues.

99
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:36,520
And you're trying to build a case.

100
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:42,160
You're trying to piece together the clues to build the most plausible case for who committed the murder and why.

101
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:46,300
There's actually, the ones that we get, there's a lot of nuance and detail.

102
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:49,020
And you could get all of the clues and unpack it and build your case.

103
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:52,540
And you're still not totally sure it's the person you think it is, right?

104
00:06:52,540 --> 00:06:57,780
Like, there's usually two or three possibilities that you really have to work through.

105
00:06:57,780 --> 00:07:04,540
And then kind of at the end say, like, pretty sure it's this person and here's all of the reasons why building the case.

106
00:07:05,380 --> 00:07:11,700
And then you have to just, by faith, plug in the answer and see if you won the game night or not.

107
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:15,940
We have yet to lose, mostly, again, because Marissa grew up on Agatha Christie.

108
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:19,140
So I'll give her all the credit for that.

109
00:07:19,140 --> 00:07:26,360
But the reason I share this story is because in a lot of ways, this is how each one of us as individuals living on a broken world,

110
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:33,580
living in a fallen world in the midst of suffering and chaos and difficulty and the grind of everyday life,

111
00:07:33,580 --> 00:07:38,580
in a lot of ways, this is how we all build the case for our own meaning and significance.

112
00:07:38,580 --> 00:07:47,260
We're just plopped into the middle of creation in our little sphere in Frederick County with all of the experiences that we have.

113
00:07:47,260 --> 00:07:49,040
And we're trying to make sense of the world.

114
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:50,020
Why are we here?

115
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:50,980
How did we get here?

116
00:07:50,980 --> 00:07:52,080
What does this all mean?

117
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:55,180
What is my purpose for being here?

118
00:07:55,680 --> 00:08:02,240
And philosophers call this idea, this building the case for your life, they call this a worldview.

119
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:04,040
And you guys went to college.

120
00:08:04,040 --> 00:08:05,780
You probably had a philosophy class about worldview.

121
00:08:05,780 --> 00:08:07,480
Am I the only one?

122
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:11,960
I know just enough about philosophy and worldview to make a fool out of myself this morning.

123
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:13,560
So this should be fun.

124
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:16,280
So whether you know it or not, we all have one.

125
00:08:16,280 --> 00:08:17,380
We all have a worldview.

126
00:08:17,380 --> 00:08:24,260
And a worldview is a set of assumptions that we as individuals have kind of put in place to interpret our life.

127
00:08:24,320 --> 00:08:32,660
It is the case we build to make sense of the details of our life, to put us into a broader story.

128
00:08:32,660 --> 00:08:42,100
It's the underlying answers we believe about the most fundamental questions of reality, like where did we come from, why are we here, and what happens to us after we die.

129
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:45,680
It helps us make sense of complex situations.

130
00:08:45,680 --> 00:08:51,580
Now, many of us in the room likely have what we would call a biblical worldview.

131
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:55,720
That's not all one size fits all, right?

132
00:08:55,720 --> 00:08:57,340
There's not just one biblical worldview.

133
00:08:57,340 --> 00:09:11,940
But most of us have some version of a biblical worldview where we look to the Scriptures, to the life and teachings of Jesus, and to creation to answer these big questions about life, to build the case for why we're here and what this whole thing is all about.

134
00:09:11,940 --> 00:09:22,780
Whether you hold to a biblical worldview or not, the reality is most of us are likely influenced by, or if you grew up in the type of church that I grew up in,

135
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:27,060
reacting against the secular worldviews of the day.

136
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:28,100
None of that is bad.

137
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:37,060
It's just important to understand that we've likely been influenced or reacting against popular worldviews of the day.

138
00:09:37,060 --> 00:09:42,660
The two most prominent that many of you would probably be familiar with is the modern worldview and the postmodern worldview.

139
00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:44,500
Somebody shake your head if you know these words.

140
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:45,680
Okay, cool.

141
00:09:46,080 --> 00:09:50,140
Some of us do, and the rest of us are going to get one of the worst summary lessons they've ever heard.

142
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:56,640
The modern worldview, it comes out of the Enlightenment and the technological revolution,

143
00:09:56,640 --> 00:10:02,600
and it puts all of its confidence in science, logic, and reason to answer life's toughest questions.

144
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:06,460
It says, we can figure out the answers to our most complex questions.

145
00:10:06,460 --> 00:10:07,880
We have it inside of us.

146
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:10,820
With science and logic and reason, we can figure it out.

147
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:13,980
This worldview is famously summarized by the quote by Nietzsche.

148
00:10:13,980 --> 00:10:16,820
He said, God is dead, and we killed him.

149
00:10:16,820 --> 00:10:22,760
This is a worldview moving away from all spiritual realm and just saying, we can figure out.

150
00:10:22,760 --> 00:10:27,260
We can figure out the answers to the questions that we need with our hands and with our minds.

151
00:10:27,260 --> 00:10:27,960
We'll figure it out.

152
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:34,800
The postmodern worldview did what many young generations are known for.

153
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:43,580
I don't know if you know this about young generations, but we swing pendulums to the extreme side of where our parents were.

154
00:10:43,580 --> 00:10:47,480
It's super healthy and great, and it always works out.

155
00:10:47,480 --> 00:10:54,060
So then it just ends up the older generation and the younger generation are just both wrong,

156
00:10:54,060 --> 00:10:56,680
and we're like yelling at each other from opposite sides of the pendulum.

157
00:10:56,860 --> 00:11:03,660
But the postmodern worldview swung the pendulum and was rightly skeptical of the overconfidence boasted by the modernists,

158
00:11:03,660 --> 00:11:06,520
and they built their worldview on skepticism.

159
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:09,640
They sought to tear down all absolute truth claims.

160
00:11:09,640 --> 00:11:11,820
Truth is a subjective experience.

161
00:11:11,820 --> 00:11:15,560
We can all make sense of our own story on our own.

162
00:11:15,560 --> 00:11:19,140
There doesn't have to be a grand narrative that makes sense of all of human existence.

163
00:11:19,140 --> 00:11:22,400
You've probably heard the phrase, live your truth.

164
00:11:22,400 --> 00:11:26,160
That is kind of the mantra of the postmodern worldview.

165
00:11:26,860 --> 00:11:33,920
Now, we live in a really interesting time where many philosophers and sociologists think that there is a crisis of worldview.

166
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:38,980
Many people, and we can know this for a couple of different reasons.

167
00:11:38,980 --> 00:11:44,780
First is many people with a biblical worldview are deconstructing their worldview,

168
00:11:44,780 --> 00:11:47,840
either completely or, and walking away from the church.

169
00:11:47,940 --> 00:11:52,680
You guys probably know, every one of us in here probably knows somebody who grew up with a biblical worldview.

170
00:11:52,680 --> 00:11:57,180
And because of the answers that they got for their toughest questions said,

171
00:11:57,180 --> 00:11:58,620
yeah, this isn't doing it for me.

172
00:11:58,620 --> 00:12:01,640
I'm going to walk away from the church altogether.

173
00:12:01,640 --> 00:12:03,920
That's kind of the worst case scenario.

174
00:12:03,920 --> 00:12:10,220
Many others are deconstructing their biblical worldview and rebuilding one that is a bit more nuanced and complex

175
00:12:10,220 --> 00:12:14,500
and can handle some of the more difficult questions of life.

176
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:20,520
And those outside the church seem to be, the younger generation seems to be discontent with the modern view

177
00:12:20,520 --> 00:12:28,280
and the postmodern view, and they're trying to find some new version of a worldview that answers their toughest question.

178
00:12:29,220 --> 00:12:34,440
Now, the reason for this brief and oversimplified philosophy lesson is to say this.

179
00:12:34,440 --> 00:12:41,680
I think that Christians and non-Christians alike are sick of oversimplified answers to nuanced questions.

180
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:47,720
We're sick of being given answers that don't satisfy the longings of our hearts.

181
00:12:47,720 --> 00:12:51,640
The overconfidence of modernism has failed us.

182
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:59,300
The subjectivity and deconstruction of postmodernism left us outside a house that we destroyed in ruins.

183
00:12:59,300 --> 00:13:04,140
And the oversimplified and sentimental biblical worldview comes up short.

184
00:13:04,140 --> 00:13:14,660
We often, as a church, we take the Bible and we use it to create oversimplified and unhelpful answers to nuanced and difficult questions.

185
00:13:14,660 --> 00:13:20,080
And people are unsatisfied with the ways in which we are answering the most difficult question.

186
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:32,740
But, as a person who started this pendulum swing in his own journey and began to deconstruct some of the unhelpful answers to questions that I found,

187
00:13:32,740 --> 00:13:38,880
the more I study, the more confident I have become that the Bible is the story that we are all looking for.

188
00:13:38,880 --> 00:13:41,280
It is complex and it is beautiful.

189
00:13:41,280 --> 00:13:46,460
It gives us the answers that we need to our most challenging questions,

190
00:13:46,460 --> 00:13:50,800
and it comforts us in the midst of the questions it doesn't sufficiently answer.

191
00:13:50,800 --> 00:13:54,860
It gives us what we need.

192
00:13:54,860 --> 00:13:57,980
It doesn't give us everything that we want, but it gives us what we need.

193
00:13:57,980 --> 00:14:00,760
And the God of all comfort meets us in the midst of our questions.

194
00:14:00,760 --> 00:14:07,320
And the book of Ruth is a summary of the entire biblical story in a singular book.

195
00:14:07,320 --> 00:14:08,840
It's a test case.

196
00:14:08,840 --> 00:14:15,260
We get a test case into this one family and we get to see them interact with the worldview of the Bible.

197
00:14:15,260 --> 00:14:22,160
As they are thrust into difficult circumstances, as they suffer and wrestle with the realities of living in a broken creation,

198
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:24,580
we get to see them wrestle with God.

199
00:14:25,580 --> 00:14:30,740
And we get to see how the grand narrative of Scripture makes sense of their suffering and their sorrow.

200
00:14:32,900 --> 00:14:37,860
It gives us a look into how the story of one family interacts with the story of the Bible,

201
00:14:37,860 --> 00:14:44,580
and it invites us to walk alongside of them and see how the story of the Bible might make sense of each one of our stories,

202
00:14:44,580 --> 00:14:47,740
each one of the difficulties of life that we face.

203
00:14:47,740 --> 00:14:56,580
Every question that we might have, we can begin to see how God interacts with Ruth and Naomi in the midst of their questioning and doubt.

204
00:14:56,640 --> 00:14:59,160
One pastor I really like says it this way.

205
00:14:59,160 --> 00:15:04,600
He says, the book of Ruth is the story of the whole Bible told through three characters in four chapters.

206
00:15:04,600 --> 00:15:13,960
It really is this grand narrative, this worldview, this biblical worldview kind of played out through the life of Ruth and Naomi and Boaz,

207
00:15:13,960 --> 00:15:15,200
which we won't get to today.

208
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:16,300
We'll get to Boaz later.

209
00:15:16,300 --> 00:15:18,880
But for now, Ruth and Naomi.

210
00:15:19,040 --> 00:15:24,620
So my goal for today is to just set the book up, to show you how it works as an overview,

211
00:15:24,620 --> 00:15:30,720
and then we'll spend the next few weeks leading up to Easter to kind of work through it in more detail.

212
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:31,560
Does that sound like a plan?

213
00:15:31,560 --> 00:15:32,780
Awesome.

214
00:15:32,780 --> 00:15:45,320
Thank you for following along with my generic value version of philosophy class this morning.

215
00:15:45,320 --> 00:15:47,780
I'm trying to think of a way to make it sound better than that.

216
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:50,100
All right, Ruth chapter one, verse one.

217
00:15:50,100 --> 00:15:56,520
In the days when the judges ruled in Israel, a severe famine came upon the land.

218
00:15:56,520 --> 00:16:02,800
So a man from Bethlehem in Judah left his home and went to live in the country of Moab,

219
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:05,480
taking his wife and his two sons with him.

220
00:16:05,480 --> 00:16:09,060
The names, the man's name was Elimelech.

221
00:16:09,060 --> 00:16:10,660
Look to your neighbor and say, Elimelech.

222
00:16:10,660 --> 00:16:13,500
It's fun to say, isn't it?

223
00:16:14,720 --> 00:16:18,660
And if you don't like your neighbor, you can accidentally spit while saying Elimelech.

224
00:16:18,660 --> 00:16:23,160
And we can see how pacifist we are in the room this morning.

225
00:16:23,160 --> 00:16:27,540
The man's name was Elimelech and his wife's name was Naomi.

226
00:16:27,540 --> 00:16:34,000
Their two sons were Malon and Kilion, and they were Epaphrodites from Bethlehem in the land of Judah.

227
00:16:35,060 --> 00:16:37,580
And when they reached Moab, they settled there.

228
00:16:37,580 --> 00:16:43,040
Then Elimelech died, and Naomi was left with her two sons.

229
00:16:43,040 --> 00:16:45,320
The two sons married Moabite women.

230
00:16:45,320 --> 00:16:49,220
One married a woman named Orpah, the other Ruth.

231
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:53,020
But about ten years later, both Malon and Kilion died.

232
00:16:53,020 --> 00:16:58,240
This left Naomi alone without her two sons or her husband.

233
00:16:58,240 --> 00:17:04,040
You guys hoping to be encouraged this morning?

234
00:17:04,040 --> 00:17:10,020
This is the reality of life in a broken world.

235
00:17:10,020 --> 00:17:14,240
And the author of Ruth doesn't waste any time to set up the plot of the story.

236
00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:18,940
We immediately see an Israelite family doing their best to survive.

237
00:17:18,940 --> 00:17:24,220
This is a family that finds themselves in the nation of God's chosen people.

238
00:17:24,220 --> 00:17:25,680
Right?

239
00:17:25,680 --> 00:17:27,800
Life should be good for God's chosen people.

240
00:17:27,800 --> 00:17:32,380
But that's not what we see in the first few verses of the book of Ruth.

241
00:17:32,380 --> 00:17:37,020
And it's often not what those of us who follow Jesus faithfully experience as our own life.

242
00:17:37,020 --> 00:17:41,940
Israelite family was doing their best to survive and struggle.

243
00:17:41,940 --> 00:17:46,820
And we're struggling in a world where life isn't going as they thought it would.

244
00:17:46,820 --> 00:17:50,040
Anybody resonate with that story?

245
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:54,400
You can see a lot of parallels.

246
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:56,000
For those of you that are familiar with Job,

247
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:59,320
there's a lot of parallels between the book of Ruth and the book of Job immediately.

248
00:17:59,320 --> 00:18:01,120
Naomi and her family.

249
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:03,560
Naomi and Ruth are left with nothing.

250
00:18:03,560 --> 00:18:08,940
Left to wrestle with God and try and figure out how to make sense of all of this.

251
00:18:09,560 --> 00:18:15,700
The author of Ruth gives us a setting to understand where we're at in the grand narrative of Scripture.

252
00:18:15,700 --> 00:18:16,280
They say,

253
00:18:16,280 --> 00:18:19,040
In the days the judges ruled.

254
00:18:19,040 --> 00:18:21,500
Again, if you went to Sunday school growing up, you know this.

255
00:18:21,500 --> 00:18:22,960
But for those of us who didn't,

256
00:18:22,960 --> 00:18:28,660
this seven-word phrase is packed with cultural context for the original recipients.

257
00:18:29,440 --> 00:18:36,220
It would be like me saying to you as I turned on the news that morning, September 12th, 2001.

258
00:18:36,220 --> 00:18:37,800
Right?

259
00:18:37,800 --> 00:18:43,380
It carries with it like cultural understanding of what was happening in that day.

260
00:18:43,380 --> 00:18:47,880
All of us that lived through that day are immediately transported back to what our experience was like.

261
00:18:47,980 --> 00:18:50,880
And those of us who are too young to be alive at that point,

262
00:18:50,880 --> 00:18:56,180
you've heard enough stories where you can, in your mind's eye, imagine yourself in that cultural setting.

263
00:18:56,180 --> 00:18:57,840
And this is what the author of Ruth does.

264
00:18:57,840 --> 00:19:01,840
She sets us in the context of the book of Judges.

265
00:19:01,840 --> 00:19:10,600
We're transported to this time where one generation after Joshua leads the nation of Israel into the promised land.

266
00:19:10,840 --> 00:19:11,260
Right?

267
00:19:11,260 --> 00:19:17,760
So we've got God bringing the Israelites out of slavery in Egypt, wandering in the wilderness for 40 years.

268
00:19:17,760 --> 00:19:21,260
Joshua brings the people of Israel into the promised land.

269
00:19:21,260 --> 00:19:25,460
And one generation after Joshua, we read this in the book of Judges,

270
00:19:25,460 --> 00:19:32,000
an entire generation grew up that did not acknowledge God or remember the things that he has done.

271
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:37,580
What do we say about younger generations swinging the pendulum?

272
00:19:37,580 --> 00:19:38,720
Right?

273
00:19:38,780 --> 00:19:44,520
We have a generation of people who wrestled with God in the wilderness and through miraculous power

274
00:19:44,520 --> 00:19:49,280
were brought into the land that they had been promised as they were waiting for 400 years in slavery.

275
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:50,640
God delivers them.

276
00:19:50,640 --> 00:19:52,840
He does the thing he promised to do.

277
00:19:52,840 --> 00:19:59,160
And one generation later, we have an entire group of people who have totally left and forsaken God,

278
00:19:59,160 --> 00:20:04,320
totally ignored what God has done for them and is doing for them.

279
00:20:04,320 --> 00:20:08,400
This was their response.

280
00:20:08,780 --> 00:20:15,120
At the very end of the book of Judges, we can read a summary statement of how this entire generation lived.

281
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:17,960
It says,

282
00:20:17,960 --> 00:20:25,160
in those days, in the days the judges ruled, Israel had no king and everyone did what was right in their own eyes.

283
00:20:25,160 --> 00:20:33,520
They ignored their relationship with God and his laws and once again began to define good and evil for themselves.

284
00:20:33,520 --> 00:20:37,440
Sound like another time in Scripture that we might be familiar with?

285
00:20:38,300 --> 00:20:38,820
Right?

286
00:20:38,820 --> 00:20:39,880
It's this cycle.

287
00:20:39,880 --> 00:20:41,580
It's this pattern of human existence.

288
00:20:41,580 --> 00:20:44,560
God shows up, invites us into relationship with himself.

289
00:20:44,560 --> 00:20:47,640
We rebel and choose to define good and evil for ourselves.

290
00:20:47,640 --> 00:20:51,480
And when we do that, chaos and suffering follow.

291
00:20:51,480 --> 00:20:56,880
They ignored their relationship with God and his laws, defined good and evil for themselves.

292
00:20:56,880 --> 00:21:03,020
They worshiped false gods and their idolatry through the entire nation into chaos and disorder.

293
00:21:03,140 --> 00:21:06,360
Throughout the book of Judges, we see what theologians call the judges cycle.

294
00:21:06,360 --> 00:21:10,240
Where they rebel, define good and evil in their own eyes.

295
00:21:10,240 --> 00:21:12,520
They do what they want to do, how they want to do it.

296
00:21:12,520 --> 00:21:14,180
Sound like anybody you're familiar with?

297
00:21:14,180 --> 00:21:16,720
We just talked about postmodernism, right?

298
00:21:16,720 --> 00:21:21,500
Postmodernism is in some ways a reliving of the cycle of the judges.

299
00:21:21,500 --> 00:21:23,080
We can do whatever we want.

300
00:21:23,080 --> 00:21:25,340
We can define good and evil for ourselves.

301
00:21:26,620 --> 00:21:27,480
How's that work?

302
00:21:27,480 --> 00:21:31,300
All right.

303
00:21:31,300 --> 00:21:32,200
Good.

304
00:21:32,200 --> 00:21:36,300
We see this cycle, right?

305
00:21:36,300 --> 00:21:39,260
We see this cycle where they do what is right in their own eyes.

306
00:21:39,260 --> 00:21:41,480
It leads to suffering and chaos and they're stubborn.

307
00:21:41,480 --> 00:21:43,700
And so they muscle it out for as long as they can.

308
00:21:43,700 --> 00:21:47,900
And then they say, okay, I guess we'll repent and we'll ask God for forgiveness.

309
00:21:47,900 --> 00:21:52,860
And he raises up a judge who delivers them from their suffering and their chaos.

310
00:21:52,860 --> 00:21:57,180
And as soon as they get comfort, as soon as they get relief from their suffering and their chaos,

311
00:21:57,180 --> 00:22:01,160
they turn back to doing whatever is right in their own eyes.

312
00:22:01,160 --> 00:22:02,860
This is the judges cycle.

313
00:22:02,860 --> 00:22:06,640
And this is the setting for the book of Ruth.

314
00:22:06,640 --> 00:22:15,680
And I think it helps us to kind of zoom out and see an underlying principle that is true of God's grand narrative.

315
00:22:15,680 --> 00:22:21,460
Remember we said the book of Ruth helps us to zoom out and see how God's story interacts with our story.

316
00:22:21,460 --> 00:22:25,160
And there is a reality that is true in God's world.

317
00:22:25,160 --> 00:22:28,800
It's true in nuanced explanation.

318
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:32,040
Some people try and oversimplify this and use it for their own good.

319
00:22:32,040 --> 00:22:36,520
But I would say this, that submission to God leads to life and flourishing.

320
00:22:36,520 --> 00:22:40,320
Rebellion against God leads to chaos and suffering.

321
00:22:40,320 --> 00:22:45,600
This is a reality of the biblical narrative from the beginning, from Genesis chapter one.

322
00:22:45,600 --> 00:22:53,340
And God is inviting people to submission to him as the Lord and leader, as the creator who deserves their worship.

323
00:22:53,340 --> 00:22:55,900
He's inviting us to partner with him.

324
00:22:55,900 --> 00:23:02,320
And when we partner with him, we get something called Eden, which is abundance and life.

325
00:23:03,380 --> 00:23:14,540
But when we choose to rebel against God, we get something called east of Eden, life outside of the Garden of Eden, which leads to chaos and suffering.

326
00:23:14,540 --> 00:23:18,580
Adam and Eve chose to rebel, which thrust the world into chaos and suffering.

327
00:23:20,260 --> 00:23:22,780
But God does not give up on the human project.

328
00:23:22,780 --> 00:23:28,860
Remember we talked about a couple weeks ago, the way that he defines himself before the people of Israel.

329
00:23:28,860 --> 00:23:34,780
He says, Yahweh is compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in steadfast love.

330
00:23:35,280 --> 00:23:45,560
It's the very character and nature of God, that he does not give up on us, that he slowly and patiently draws us back into relationship with himself, inviting us to partner with him.

331
00:23:45,560 --> 00:23:48,960
And when we choose to partner with him, we get life and flourishing.

332
00:23:48,960 --> 00:23:55,700
Now, this is where I want to say that it's nuanced, because this is not prosperity gospel stuff, right?

333
00:23:55,700 --> 00:24:00,920
This is not do the right thing, give the right amount of money, pray the right prayers, and everything in life goes well.

334
00:24:01,600 --> 00:24:12,660
This is more like what Jesus defines as abundant life, and he tells his disciples who are about to be martyred for following him, that they can have abundant life if they trust him.

335
00:24:12,660 --> 00:24:18,580
So abundant life does not necessarily mean what we think when we think life and flourishing.

336
00:24:18,580 --> 00:24:27,320
But if we can, by faith, just cling to the God who defines good and evil,

337
00:24:27,700 --> 00:24:32,700
if we can repent from our original decision to define good and evil for ourselves,

338
00:24:32,700 --> 00:24:36,580
and trust that he knows what he's doing when he says,

339
00:24:36,580 --> 00:24:39,320
trust me and it will lead to life flourishing,

340
00:24:39,320 --> 00:24:47,480
then just maybe we can get some reprieve, some redemption in the midst of our suffering.

341
00:24:52,700 --> 00:24:58,420
The nation of Israel is this partnership between a person and God.

342
00:24:58,420 --> 00:25:06,980
God invites Abraham to partner with him, to submit to him, and to have life and abundance in his name.

343
00:25:06,980 --> 00:25:12,840
In the book of Genesis, everyone is moving away from Eden.

344
00:25:12,840 --> 00:25:17,720
As you read Genesis 1 through 11, like every single person is moving away from God,

345
00:25:17,880 --> 00:25:21,460
eastward, away from Eden, and God grabs Abraham and he says,

346
00:25:21,460 --> 00:25:23,460
will you trust me and will you move west?

347
00:25:23,460 --> 00:25:25,420
Will you come back with me?

348
00:25:25,420 --> 00:25:27,740
And if you will, I will create a nation for you.

349
00:25:27,740 --> 00:25:31,580
And this nation will be this project of human partnership with God.

350
00:25:31,580 --> 00:25:36,000
And in some ways, it's Adam and Eve's partnership with God restored for the nation of Israel.

351
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:41,660
And he's going to set up the tabernacle, which becomes like a miniature version of Eden in the midst of Israel.

352
00:25:41,660 --> 00:25:46,980
And he's saying that if you partner with me, if you trust me, I will bring about Eden,

353
00:25:46,980 --> 00:25:50,460
even in the midst of chaos, in the chaos of your life.

354
00:25:50,460 --> 00:25:55,760
It doesn't mean the chaos outside goes away.

355
00:25:55,760 --> 00:25:59,020
It means that he brings Eden to us in the midst of our chaos.

356
00:26:01,520 --> 00:26:04,540
So as God brought the nation of Israel into their promised land,

357
00:26:04,540 --> 00:26:06,660
he set up some ground rules for their partnership.

358
00:26:06,660 --> 00:26:10,520
In Deuteronomy chapter 28, he says,

359
00:26:10,520 --> 00:26:13,280
if you obey me, you will experience blessing and abundance.

360
00:26:13,280 --> 00:26:14,460
You will experience Eden.

361
00:26:14,460 --> 00:26:17,640
If you disobey me, you will experience judgment and famine.

362
00:26:17,640 --> 00:26:20,680
All right.

363
00:26:20,680 --> 00:26:25,420
Now, the worst reading of this, for those of you that are skeptical about a biblical worldview,

364
00:26:25,420 --> 00:26:30,080
the worst reading of this turns God into a cosmic dictator in the sky on a power trip.

365
00:26:31,360 --> 00:26:35,520
Anybody have friends who would say they read the Old Testament and that's the God they see?

366
00:26:35,520 --> 00:26:36,600
Okay.

367
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:41,340
I don't necessarily, like, judge them for that.

368
00:26:41,340 --> 00:26:45,120
Let's help them, again, give a nuanced explanation of what's going on.

369
00:26:45,120 --> 00:26:50,900
But a proper reading is that God created everything and knows how creation is intended to function.

370
00:26:50,900 --> 00:26:53,400
His laws represent and summarize this.

371
00:26:53,400 --> 00:26:57,360
His law is summarized by love of God and love of neighbor.

372
00:26:58,180 --> 00:27:01,540
And if you really dig into God's law, as we will throughout the book of Ruth,

373
00:27:01,540 --> 00:27:09,780
you will see that the laws that he gave the people of Israel run in contrary to the power structures of this world that lead to chaos and death.

374
00:27:09,780 --> 00:27:17,360
The law is summarized and pays special attention to the most vulnerable people.

375
00:27:18,060 --> 00:27:22,200
And it invites leadership that is rooted in humility and sacrificial service.

376
00:27:22,200 --> 00:27:30,320
If you read the description of kings that God gives in Deuteronomy, of the type of king that should rule Israel,

377
00:27:30,320 --> 00:27:35,000
it's a king who isn't collecting a bunch of power, who isn't collecting a bunch of money,

378
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:42,300
but who is distributing wealth to God's people so that they can be a testimony to who God is to the rest of the world.

379
00:27:42,300 --> 00:27:47,420
It runs in contrary to how we run nations today.

380
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:54,480
The way I always illustrated this for my youth group students was, it was always fun.

381
00:27:54,480 --> 00:28:02,160
I would have a board, a two by four, with a couple of nails, and I would have them race to hammer the nail into the board.

382
00:28:02,160 --> 00:28:03,480
One of them would get a hammer.

383
00:28:03,480 --> 00:28:05,560
Another would get like a boulder.

384
00:28:05,560 --> 00:28:07,800
Another would get a brick, right?

385
00:28:07,800 --> 00:28:10,680
And it's like, okay, like, go, race.

386
00:28:10,680 --> 00:28:12,420
Try to get this nail in this board.

387
00:28:13,660 --> 00:28:15,300
You guys know what happened, right?

388
00:28:15,300 --> 00:28:17,220
The person with the hammer won.

389
00:28:17,220 --> 00:28:23,860
And the person with the rock and the brick sometimes bruised the fingernail, cut open their skin.

390
00:28:23,860 --> 00:28:24,900
Like, it was awesome.

391
00:28:24,900 --> 00:28:26,400
I got lots of calls from parents.

392
00:28:26,400 --> 00:28:32,340
But the reality is, like, God created us.

393
00:28:32,340 --> 00:28:34,080
He knows how life is intended to work.

394
00:28:34,080 --> 00:28:37,600
And when he gives us tools and definitions for right and wrong,

395
00:28:37,600 --> 00:28:40,500
it's not because he's a cosmic dictator on a power trip,

396
00:28:40,500 --> 00:28:42,620
but it's because he knows how the thing works.

397
00:28:43,660 --> 00:28:47,540
And he's invited us to partner with him and do it the way that he's called us to do it.

398
00:28:47,540 --> 00:28:49,100
And we can do it in other ways.

399
00:28:49,100 --> 00:28:51,560
We can live life in other ways.

400
00:28:51,560 --> 00:28:55,820
And, like, you can hammer a nail with a rock or with a brick,

401
00:28:55,820 --> 00:28:58,280
but it often does some damage to you.

402
00:28:58,280 --> 00:28:59,860
It does some damage to the wood.

403
00:28:59,860 --> 00:29:01,120
It does some damage to the nail.

404
00:29:01,120 --> 00:29:07,240
Like, there are consequences for doing things in a way that is not in line with the design.

405
00:29:07,240 --> 00:29:10,320
And that's how creation functions.

406
00:29:10,320 --> 00:29:13,640
After the fall, the nation of Israel is functioning,

407
00:29:13,660 --> 00:29:15,260
this way in the time of judges.

408
00:29:15,260 --> 00:29:19,420
They are trying to cultivate God's creation in a way that leads to life and flourishing,

409
00:29:19,420 --> 00:29:23,400
but they are using their own definitions, their own tools, and their own strength.

410
00:29:23,400 --> 00:29:26,460
And this will always ultimately lead to chaos and suffering.

411
00:29:26,460 --> 00:29:30,580
We see that immediately in this text.

412
00:29:30,580 --> 00:29:33,720
Israel had a severe famine come upon the land.

413
00:29:33,720 --> 00:29:36,940
This family in Bethlehem had no food.

414
00:29:37,660 --> 00:29:42,200
The land of Israel was described as this land flowing with milk and honey.

415
00:29:42,200 --> 00:29:46,480
The name Bethlehem literally means the house of bread.

416
00:29:46,480 --> 00:29:52,200
And so because God's people rebelled against God and chose to live life their own way,

417
00:29:52,200 --> 00:29:54,500
based on their own definitions and their own understanding,

418
00:29:54,500 --> 00:29:59,400
now we go from them being brought into the land flowing with milk and honey,

419
00:29:59,400 --> 00:30:00,760
into the house of bread,

420
00:30:00,760 --> 00:30:03,400
and now we see there is no food in the house of bread,

421
00:30:03,400 --> 00:30:05,320
in the land flowing with milk and honey.

422
00:30:05,320 --> 00:30:09,460
This is life outside of Eden.

423
00:30:09,460 --> 00:30:14,160
This is creation experiencing the consequences of living apart from God.

424
00:30:14,160 --> 00:30:18,880
Now there's, again, to be nuanced,

425
00:30:18,880 --> 00:30:24,120
there's three major categories of chaos and suffering that all of us face from time to time.

426
00:30:24,620 --> 00:30:29,480
The first is our consequences of decisions that we have made ourselves.

427
00:30:29,480 --> 00:30:33,880
All of us at times rebel against God.

428
00:30:33,880 --> 00:30:35,700
We choose right and wrong for ourselves.

429
00:30:35,700 --> 00:30:40,820
We make decisions apart from God and we suffer the consequences of those decisions.

430
00:30:40,820 --> 00:30:46,520
The second is many of us know the pain of suffering consequences from decisions of others.

431
00:30:46,520 --> 00:30:50,680
Somebody else decides to do something that is in rebellion to God,

432
00:30:50,680 --> 00:30:51,720
that apart from God,

433
00:30:51,720 --> 00:30:55,760
and they're in proximity to you and that decision leads to your chaos and suffering.

434
00:30:55,760 --> 00:31:01,720
Those of you with parents who have substance abuse understand this type of suffering.

435
00:31:01,720 --> 00:31:07,300
Those of you who have experienced sexual abuse as children understand this type of suffering.

436
00:31:07,300 --> 00:31:11,260
Many of us in the room have suffered because of the consequences of others.

437
00:31:11,260 --> 00:31:13,060
Poverty, abuse, racism, injustice,

438
00:31:13,060 --> 00:31:17,300
all of this is suffering that we have experienced because of the decision of others.

439
00:31:17,300 --> 00:31:21,540
And the last and the one that's the hardest for all of us to reconcile,

440
00:31:21,720 --> 00:31:24,600
is living in a broken world.

441
00:31:24,600 --> 00:31:33,060
Some of us just suffer what seems indiscriminate suffering because we live in a broken world.

442
00:31:33,060 --> 00:31:37,800
Natural disasters, mental health struggles, cancer, infertility, and on and on we could go.

443
00:31:37,800 --> 00:31:45,120
There's something that just doesn't make sense about good people suffering consequences in this world.

444
00:31:46,020 --> 00:31:50,900
And the reality is all of us in this room have likely suffered one or many of these.

445
00:31:50,900 --> 00:31:59,500
However, I think the temptation we often face when suffering any of these is to turn our pain and anger towards God.

446
00:32:01,060 --> 00:32:03,840
We want Him to erase the consequences of our mistake.

447
00:32:03,840 --> 00:32:05,700
Totally, completely, scot-free.

448
00:32:05,700 --> 00:32:07,940
God, I'm sorry, eliminate it.

449
00:32:07,940 --> 00:32:12,440
But, how do we want God to treat people who have sinned against us?

450
00:32:12,440 --> 00:32:17,480
Swift and merciless judgment to those who have sinned against us.

451
00:32:18,620 --> 00:32:27,040
And we can't, we cannot reconcile how a good and powerful God would seemingly let indiscriminate suffering fall upon His creation.

452
00:32:27,040 --> 00:32:32,300
That's why I had Josh read that passage in 2 Peter.

453
00:32:32,300 --> 00:32:39,280
Because I think what we see as God, a good and powerful God, letting indiscriminate suffering happen,

454
00:32:39,280 --> 00:32:43,520
is actually God's patient invitation to all of His creation.

455
00:32:43,740 --> 00:32:47,240
Yes, we live in a broken world where there is suffering that doesn't always make sense.

456
00:32:47,240 --> 00:32:51,300
But the reason it's dragging out this long is because God is compassionate and gracious.

457
00:32:51,300 --> 00:32:53,980
He's slow to anger and He's abounding in steadfast love.

458
00:32:53,980 --> 00:32:58,020
And He's inviting more and more people to surrender their lives,

459
00:32:58,020 --> 00:33:02,300
to give up on their own definitions of good and evil, and to turn to Him.

460
00:33:02,300 --> 00:33:06,600
And so sometimes good people, people who are faithful to God,

461
00:33:06,600 --> 00:33:09,480
they suffer in the midst of the brokenness of this world.

462
00:33:09,480 --> 00:33:10,500
And you're like, why?

463
00:33:10,500 --> 00:33:11,780
They didn't do anything.

464
00:33:11,780 --> 00:33:13,080
Nothing was done to them.

465
00:33:14,360 --> 00:33:19,300
But what if God is just patiently inviting us to surrender to Him,

466
00:33:19,300 --> 00:33:21,300
to trust Him, and to wait for His redemption?

467
00:33:21,300 --> 00:33:25,520
Which leads me to my next logical question,

468
00:33:25,520 --> 00:33:28,420
and I have to move fast because I'm going way slower than I intended to go.

469
00:33:28,420 --> 00:33:31,320
What about Elimelech suffering?

470
00:33:31,320 --> 00:33:33,360
Look at your neighbor again and say Elimelech,

471
00:33:33,360 --> 00:33:34,680
and spit in their face if you don't like them.

472
00:33:34,680 --> 00:33:35,560
Kidding.

473
00:33:35,560 --> 00:33:36,460
Don't do that.

474
00:33:36,460 --> 00:33:37,120
Don't do that.

475
00:33:39,040 --> 00:33:41,900
Is Elimelech suffering because of decisions that he made,

476
00:33:41,900 --> 00:33:43,540
or because of the decisions of others,

477
00:33:43,540 --> 00:33:46,700
or just because he lives in a fallen, broken world?

478
00:33:46,700 --> 00:33:51,000
Now, this is where, again, I think we can begin to see the complexities

479
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:53,260
of how the biblical story interacts with our story.

480
00:33:53,260 --> 00:33:56,040
Because I think it'd be really easy to say,

481
00:33:56,040 --> 00:33:59,540
well, Elimelech's obviously suffering the consequences of his own decisions.

482
00:34:00,080 --> 00:34:00,400
Right?

483
00:34:00,400 --> 00:34:01,340
He lives in Israel.

484
00:34:01,340 --> 00:34:05,180
Everyone in Israel is doing what's right in their own eyes,

485
00:34:05,180 --> 00:34:08,480
and that is leading to suffering and pain.

486
00:34:08,480 --> 00:34:12,100
I, however, and I could be wrong,

487
00:34:12,100 --> 00:34:13,580
I heard a pastor once say,

488
00:34:13,580 --> 00:34:16,740
this is not helping me speed up when I say things that aren't in my notes,

489
00:34:16,740 --> 00:34:21,800
but I heard a pastor say once that he thinks like 80 to 90%

490
00:34:21,800 --> 00:34:23,560
of everything he says on Sundays are right,

491
00:34:23,560 --> 00:34:26,020
and the other 10 to 20% is wrong.

492
00:34:26,020 --> 00:34:29,480
The problem is he's just not sure which percentage is wrong,

493
00:34:29,480 --> 00:34:30,440
or he wouldn't say it.

494
00:34:30,440 --> 00:34:34,100
So, I could be wrong here, but I have a theory,

495
00:34:34,100 --> 00:34:38,280
and my theory is that I don't think Elimelech and his family's story

496
00:34:38,280 --> 00:34:40,320
is as simple as we like to make it look.

497
00:34:40,320 --> 00:34:44,420
See, people look at Elimelech's story,

498
00:34:44,420 --> 00:34:45,980
and they say, okay, he's in the nation of Israel.

499
00:34:45,980 --> 00:34:48,540
Everyone is doing what's wrong, or what's right in their own eyes,

500
00:34:48,540 --> 00:34:51,700
and that's why they're suffering the consequences of life,

501
00:34:51,800 --> 00:34:53,500
in a broken world.

502
00:34:53,500 --> 00:34:57,060
But I think it's possible that up to this point,

503
00:34:57,060 --> 00:34:58,760
up to the point where they had a famine,

504
00:34:58,760 --> 00:35:01,800
I think it's actually likely, even probable,

505
00:35:01,800 --> 00:35:05,220
that Elimelech and his family were a part of a minority of Israelites

506
00:35:05,220 --> 00:35:07,200
that remained faithful and obedient to God

507
00:35:07,200 --> 00:35:09,220
in the midst of the nation in rebellion.

508
00:35:09,220 --> 00:35:13,080
Like, you read the words,

509
00:35:13,080 --> 00:35:16,600
everyone in Israel did what was right in their own eyes,

510
00:35:16,600 --> 00:35:18,620
and like, that's a literary device, right?

511
00:35:18,620 --> 00:35:20,800
That doesn't actually mean every single individual,

512
00:35:20,940 --> 00:35:23,520
there's not a single faithful person in the nation of Israel

513
00:35:23,520 --> 00:35:24,400
in the story of Judges.

514
00:35:24,400 --> 00:35:25,020
That's not true.

515
00:35:25,020 --> 00:35:27,260
I think about the story of Elijah, right?

516
00:35:27,260 --> 00:35:29,380
Where Elijah goes to battle with the prophets of Baal,

517
00:35:29,380 --> 00:35:31,560
and he wins, and he has this victory,

518
00:35:31,560 --> 00:35:33,620
and then he's on the run from his enemy,

519
00:35:33,620 --> 00:35:34,580
and he's confronted God.

520
00:35:34,580 --> 00:35:37,100
He's like, God, I am the only person in Israel

521
00:35:37,100 --> 00:35:38,260
who remained faithful to you.

522
00:35:38,260 --> 00:35:41,280
Sometimes it feels like that,

523
00:35:41,280 --> 00:35:42,460
but God laughs at him as like,

524
00:35:42,460 --> 00:35:44,040
yeah, I got like 7,000 other people

525
00:35:44,040 --> 00:35:45,400
who haven't bent the knee to the Baal.

526
00:35:45,400 --> 00:35:46,560
Right?

527
00:35:46,560 --> 00:35:47,620
Like, it's not just you.

528
00:35:47,660 --> 00:35:49,100
I know it feels like just you.

529
00:35:49,100 --> 00:35:53,400
And so I think that there's a minority group of people

530
00:35:53,400 --> 00:35:56,240
in the time of Judges who remained faithful to God.

531
00:35:56,240 --> 00:35:57,960
And the reason I think that's true about Elimelech

532
00:35:57,960 --> 00:36:00,380
is one, because the meaning of his name.

533
00:36:00,380 --> 00:36:02,420
Elimelech's name means,

534
00:36:02,420 --> 00:36:04,340
my God is king.

535
00:36:04,800 --> 00:36:07,920
And if you look at every other name in the book of Ruth,

536
00:36:07,920 --> 00:36:11,300
it's spot on with the character that they play in the story.

537
00:36:11,300 --> 00:36:15,360
It's like identical to what they do in the story.

538
00:36:15,360 --> 00:36:20,120
Some examples, Malone and Kilion died in the first five verses.

539
00:36:20,120 --> 00:36:21,180
You want to know what their names mean?

540
00:36:21,180 --> 00:36:23,760
Sicko and done for.

541
00:36:23,760 --> 00:36:27,100
Right?

542
00:36:28,540 --> 00:36:30,560
Naomi, her name means refreshment,

543
00:36:30,560 --> 00:36:35,040
and it's a synonym of Eden.

544
00:36:35,040 --> 00:36:38,860
And later, after Naomi comes back to Israel

545
00:36:38,860 --> 00:36:39,780
and she wrestles with God,

546
00:36:39,780 --> 00:36:41,480
she tells her friends,

547
00:36:41,480 --> 00:36:42,220
don't call me Naomi,

548
00:36:42,220 --> 00:36:43,140
don't call me refreshment,

549
00:36:43,140 --> 00:36:43,880
don't call me Eden,

550
00:36:43,880 --> 00:36:45,180
call me Mara,

551
00:36:45,180 --> 00:36:45,940
call me suffering,

552
00:36:45,940 --> 00:36:46,820
call me chaos.

553
00:36:46,820 --> 00:36:47,780
Right?

554
00:36:47,780 --> 00:36:49,480
So she's playing on this thing.

555
00:36:49,480 --> 00:36:52,280
The names in the story give a lot of meaning.

556
00:36:52,280 --> 00:36:54,240
Ruth means friendship.

557
00:36:54,980 --> 00:36:56,900
And as we are going to see throughout the book,

558
00:36:56,900 --> 00:37:00,060
Ruth is an incredible friend to Naomi.

559
00:37:00,060 --> 00:37:01,440
So I think Elimelech's name,

560
00:37:01,440 --> 00:37:02,440
meaning my God is king,

561
00:37:02,440 --> 00:37:03,660
demonstrates to me,

562
00:37:03,660 --> 00:37:04,720
at least in this story,

563
00:37:04,720 --> 00:37:07,260
that it's possible he's remaining faithful to God

564
00:37:07,260 --> 00:37:10,420
up to the point of this famine.

565
00:37:10,420 --> 00:37:13,180
The other thing that tips it off to me

566
00:37:13,180 --> 00:37:14,200
is throughout the book of Ruth,

567
00:37:14,200 --> 00:37:14,960
you're going to see Ruth

568
00:37:14,960 --> 00:37:17,760
have a tremendous amount of knowledge

569
00:37:17,760 --> 00:37:19,640
about the law and character of God.

570
00:37:19,640 --> 00:37:22,560
And Ruth is a Gentile woman

571
00:37:22,560 --> 00:37:24,940
who grew up with no experience

572
00:37:24,940 --> 00:37:26,360
or understanding of God's work.

573
00:37:26,360 --> 00:37:29,800
She lived in Elimelech's household for 10 years.

574
00:37:29,800 --> 00:37:30,980
She comes back to Israel

575
00:37:30,980 --> 00:37:33,020
and demonstrates an incredible amount of knowledge

576
00:37:33,020 --> 00:37:33,760
about God's work,

577
00:37:33,760 --> 00:37:35,440
about the character of God.

578
00:37:35,440 --> 00:37:36,300
And so I think,

579
00:37:36,300 --> 00:37:39,300
I think Elimelech is doing his best

580
00:37:39,300 --> 00:37:40,520
to remain faithful to God

581
00:37:40,520 --> 00:37:41,400
amidst the generation

582
00:37:41,400 --> 00:37:44,000
that is totally rebellious to God.

583
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:46,340
But when his back is against the wall,

584
00:37:46,340 --> 00:37:48,720
when his family has no food to eat,

585
00:37:48,720 --> 00:37:50,640
he gives in.

586
00:37:50,640 --> 00:37:53,060
He makes a practical decision

587
00:37:53,060 --> 00:37:54,860
over a decision that aligns

588
00:37:54,860 --> 00:37:55,960
with his spiritual identity.

589
00:37:55,960 --> 00:38:00,580
And I can't help but wonder.

590
00:38:00,580 --> 00:38:03,140
See, Moab is a place

591
00:38:03,140 --> 00:38:05,400
that the Israelites had no business going to.

592
00:38:05,400 --> 00:38:07,560
No matter how bad it was,

593
00:38:07,560 --> 00:38:08,760
God was inviting them

594
00:38:08,760 --> 00:38:10,300
to live in the promised land.

595
00:38:10,300 --> 00:38:13,760
And Moab and Israel don't mix.

596
00:38:13,760 --> 00:38:16,600
It's like going to your nation's enemy,

597
00:38:16,600 --> 00:38:18,200
a nation that is totally,

598
00:38:18,200 --> 00:38:20,420
totally idolatrous

599
00:38:20,420 --> 00:38:22,060
and has nothing to do with the God of Israel.

600
00:38:22,060 --> 00:38:23,820
And so Elimelech makes this decision,

601
00:38:23,820 --> 00:38:25,180
this practical decision

602
00:38:25,180 --> 00:38:28,740
to try and keep his family alive.

603
00:38:28,740 --> 00:38:30,820
And I think he turns his back

604
00:38:30,820 --> 00:38:32,440
on his spiritual identity.

605
00:38:32,440 --> 00:38:35,100
I can't help but wonder

606
00:38:35,100 --> 00:38:36,280
how many of us have done

607
00:38:36,280 --> 00:38:37,740
or are doing similar things.

608
00:38:37,740 --> 00:38:41,460
We would say Jesus is the Lord of our lives,

609
00:38:41,460 --> 00:38:43,140
but if you look at the decisions we make

610
00:38:43,140 --> 00:38:44,520
when our backs are against the wall

611
00:38:44,520 --> 00:38:46,820
or when we're facing difficulty,

612
00:38:46,820 --> 00:38:49,000
they demonstrate that we value our own comfort,

613
00:38:49,820 --> 00:38:50,880
the opinions of others

614
00:38:50,880 --> 00:38:52,220
or the pressure to give our kids

615
00:38:52,220 --> 00:38:53,700
the same life their friends have

616
00:38:53,700 --> 00:38:55,700
more than we do submitting to

617
00:38:55,700 --> 00:38:56,600
and trusting Jesus

618
00:38:56,600 --> 00:38:58,420
as the Lord of our lives and our family.

619
00:38:58,420 --> 00:39:03,100
Elimelech demonstrates half-hearted obedience.

620
00:39:03,100 --> 00:39:06,120
And that half-hearted obedience

621
00:39:06,120 --> 00:39:08,300
is so true of so many of us.

622
00:39:08,300 --> 00:39:10,540
And what's disheartening to me

623
00:39:10,540 --> 00:39:14,060
is that his sons pick up on his value system.

624
00:39:14,060 --> 00:39:17,060
They, in turn, make pragmatic decisions

625
00:39:17,060 --> 00:39:17,960
when in Moab,

626
00:39:18,600 --> 00:39:20,180
when it's time for them to get married,

627
00:39:20,180 --> 00:39:21,880
when they should be marrying Israelite women,

628
00:39:21,880 --> 00:39:24,920
the law actually commands Israelite men

629
00:39:24,920 --> 00:39:26,240
not to marry Moabite women.

630
00:39:26,240 --> 00:39:28,700
The children make a pragmatic decision.

631
00:39:28,700 --> 00:39:30,560
There's no need for them to walk back to Israel

632
00:39:30,560 --> 00:39:31,240
to find wives.

633
00:39:31,240 --> 00:39:32,640
They'll just find wives in Moab.

634
00:39:32,640 --> 00:39:35,440
Fathers, mothers,

635
00:39:35,440 --> 00:39:38,500
the value system that we model

636
00:39:38,500 --> 00:39:39,900
and demonstrate to our children

637
00:39:39,900 --> 00:39:41,680
will carry into their lives.

638
00:39:42,500 --> 00:39:45,260
Do we just say that Jesus is the Lord of our lives?

639
00:39:45,260 --> 00:39:47,460
Or do we actually live that way

640
00:39:47,460 --> 00:39:49,460
even when our backs are up against the wall?

641
00:39:49,460 --> 00:39:52,900
That was not the main point of my sermon

642
00:39:52,900 --> 00:39:54,200
and we are almost done.

643
00:39:54,200 --> 00:39:55,940
So let's keep going.

644
00:39:55,940 --> 00:39:57,000
If we zoom out a bit,

645
00:39:57,000 --> 00:39:59,440
we can begin to see how the story of Ruth

646
00:39:59,440 --> 00:40:01,280
is more than just the story of one family,

647
00:40:01,280 --> 00:40:03,500
but the story of the whole Bible.

648
00:40:04,640 --> 00:40:06,980
We see from the beginning pages of Ruth,

649
00:40:06,980 --> 00:40:08,660
there's rebellion against God

650
00:40:08,660 --> 00:40:10,660
that leads to suffering and death.

651
00:40:10,660 --> 00:40:13,000
It actually leads to three primary consequences.

652
00:40:13,000 --> 00:40:16,700
If you read the first five verses of Ruth again,

653
00:40:16,700 --> 00:40:18,720
you will see that there is a struggle for food,

654
00:40:18,720 --> 00:40:20,740
a struggle for family,

655
00:40:20,740 --> 00:40:22,120
and death.

656
00:40:24,600 --> 00:40:25,500
Bible scholars,

657
00:40:25,500 --> 00:40:28,100
anybody remember those three consequences?

658
00:40:28,100 --> 00:40:31,560
Genesis 3.

659
00:40:31,560 --> 00:40:35,160
These are the three primary consequences

660
00:40:35,160 --> 00:40:36,580
of rebellion against God.

661
00:40:36,580 --> 00:40:38,200
From the beginning to now,

662
00:40:38,200 --> 00:40:39,460
it keeps happening.

663
00:40:39,460 --> 00:40:41,060
When we rebel against God,

664
00:40:41,060 --> 00:40:42,080
we'll have a struggle for food,

665
00:40:42,080 --> 00:40:43,200
we'll have a struggle for family,

666
00:40:43,200 --> 00:40:45,360
and we'll have death itself.

667
00:40:45,360 --> 00:40:47,980
This is the story of human experience.

668
00:40:47,980 --> 00:40:49,000
If you live long enough,

669
00:40:49,000 --> 00:40:50,380
you will wrestle with all three.

670
00:40:50,380 --> 00:40:52,280
Throughout the story of Ruth

671
00:40:52,280 --> 00:40:52,920
and the Bible,

672
00:40:52,920 --> 00:40:55,500
God is inviting people out of this cycle.

673
00:40:55,500 --> 00:40:58,740
We don't have to keep wrestling with God

674
00:40:58,740 --> 00:41:00,320
or doing what is right in our own eyes.

675
00:41:00,320 --> 00:41:02,200
We can trust him to redeem us

676
00:41:02,200 --> 00:41:03,380
through the midst of our suffering

677
00:41:03,380 --> 00:41:04,860
and chaos in this broken world.

678
00:41:04,860 --> 00:41:06,860
This is my main point

679
00:41:06,860 --> 00:41:09,140
that I got to way too late.

680
00:41:09,140 --> 00:41:12,240
God will bring redemption

681
00:41:12,240 --> 00:41:14,700
in the midst of chaos and suffering

682
00:41:14,700 --> 00:41:16,400
to anyone who clings to him in faith.

683
00:41:16,400 --> 00:41:18,540
This is the story of Ruth.

684
00:41:18,540 --> 00:41:20,360
This is the story of the Bible.

685
00:41:20,360 --> 00:41:22,100
This is the invitation of God.

686
00:41:22,660 --> 00:41:24,200
We live in a broken world

687
00:41:24,200 --> 00:41:25,580
with chaos and suffering,

688
00:41:25,580 --> 00:41:27,200
some of which we brought on ourselves,

689
00:41:27,200 --> 00:41:29,220
some of which are brought on by others,

690
00:41:29,220 --> 00:41:30,420
and some of which are just

691
00:41:30,420 --> 00:41:35,020
the seemingly unfortunate things

692
00:41:35,020 --> 00:41:38,060
that happen when we live in a broken world.

693
00:41:38,060 --> 00:41:41,200
Now, this is a great main point for a sermon,

694
00:41:41,200 --> 00:41:42,460
if I do say so myself.

695
00:41:42,460 --> 00:41:46,940
The problem is it's oversimplified, right?

696
00:41:46,940 --> 00:41:48,140
These are the type of answers

697
00:41:48,140 --> 00:41:49,700
that we have given people forever,

698
00:41:49,900 --> 00:41:51,520
and it's the reason why so many people

699
00:41:51,520 --> 00:41:53,320
have walked away from the story of Scripture

700
00:41:53,320 --> 00:41:56,940
because we haven't allowed the nuance to play out.

701
00:41:56,940 --> 00:41:58,700
And that's why I love the book of Ruth

702
00:41:58,700 --> 00:42:01,080
because even though we see at the beginning

703
00:42:01,080 --> 00:42:01,860
and the end of Ruth

704
00:42:01,860 --> 00:42:04,020
that this is the ultimate grand narrative,

705
00:42:04,020 --> 00:42:06,560
it does not erase the suffering in the middle.

706
00:42:06,560 --> 00:42:08,580
It does not explain it away.

707
00:42:08,580 --> 00:42:10,480
The grand narrative of Scripture,

708
00:42:10,480 --> 00:42:11,740
the redemption of God,

709
00:42:11,740 --> 00:42:13,560
does not explain away our suffering

710
00:42:13,560 --> 00:42:15,360
or try and tell you not to feel it

711
00:42:15,360 --> 00:42:17,800
or not to hate it.

712
00:42:17,800 --> 00:42:20,480
God grieves in the midst of the suffering

713
00:42:20,480 --> 00:42:22,380
of his broken creation.

714
00:42:22,380 --> 00:42:25,140
It is not something that is wrong for us

715
00:42:25,140 --> 00:42:27,000
to grieve or be angry with

716
00:42:27,000 --> 00:42:28,020
or be frustrated by.

717
00:42:28,020 --> 00:42:30,480
I heard one Jewish scholar say it this way,

718
00:42:30,480 --> 00:42:31,400
in the Jewish culture,

719
00:42:31,400 --> 00:42:33,820
questions are as sacred as answers

720
00:42:33,820 --> 00:42:36,000
because they are confident

721
00:42:36,000 --> 00:42:37,180
that the biblical narrative

722
00:42:37,180 --> 00:42:39,280
is strong enough to be tried and tested.

723
00:42:39,280 --> 00:42:41,280
We have to get better at this church.

724
00:42:41,540 --> 00:42:43,360
We have to allow people who are wrestling

725
00:42:43,360 --> 00:42:44,720
with the suffering and chaos

726
00:42:44,720 --> 00:42:46,120
of living in a broken world

727
00:42:46,120 --> 00:42:48,140
with people who have suffered the consequences

728
00:42:48,140 --> 00:42:49,640
of their own horrible decisions,

729
00:42:49,640 --> 00:42:51,060
the horrible decisions of others

730
00:42:51,060 --> 00:42:52,940
or the horrible effects

731
00:42:52,940 --> 00:42:53,980
of living in a broken world,

732
00:42:53,980 --> 00:42:55,720
we have to let them wrestle.

733
00:42:55,720 --> 00:43:00,420
We cannot offer trivial answers

734
00:43:00,420 --> 00:43:02,360
to difficult, nuanced questions.

735
00:43:02,360 --> 00:43:05,460
We have to sit with them in their suffering

736
00:43:05,460 --> 00:43:09,140
and allow them to wrestle with God

737
00:43:09,140 --> 00:43:10,020
in the midst of it.

738
00:43:10,500 --> 00:43:12,060
Yes, God will bring redemption,

739
00:43:12,060 --> 00:43:14,020
but that redemption doesn't look like

740
00:43:14,020 --> 00:43:15,200
all of our pain and suffering

741
00:43:15,200 --> 00:43:16,100
goes away forever

742
00:43:16,100 --> 00:43:18,680
and we never feel the consequences of it.

743
00:43:18,680 --> 00:43:22,900
Often redemption is painstakingly slow.

744
00:43:22,900 --> 00:43:26,060
Sometimes we don't even see it

745
00:43:26,060 --> 00:43:27,160
on this side of creation.

746
00:43:27,160 --> 00:43:29,680
Sometimes it's only redemption in eternity.

747
00:43:31,320 --> 00:43:36,880
And how when we sell people a gospel

748
00:43:36,880 --> 00:43:40,000
that doesn't let them wrestle or suffer,

749
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:42,440
that tries to give quick, short, easy answers

750
00:43:42,440 --> 00:43:44,620
to the most complex questions in their life,

751
00:43:44,620 --> 00:43:48,240
no wonder that they're walking out the doors in droves.

752
00:43:48,240 --> 00:43:51,820
We need to learn to be a people

753
00:43:51,820 --> 00:43:54,800
who create space for people to wrestle,

754
00:43:54,800 --> 00:43:56,460
to ask questions,

755
00:43:56,460 --> 00:43:58,580
because the grand narrative of Scripture

756
00:43:58,580 --> 00:44:00,000
will hold up to the test.

757
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:02,800
We can hold two truths in tension.

758
00:44:02,800 --> 00:44:04,400
We can cling to God in faith,

759
00:44:04,400 --> 00:44:06,880
looking for and partnering with Him

760
00:44:06,880 --> 00:44:07,540
in His redemption

761
00:44:07,540 --> 00:44:08,920
while continuing to wrestle

762
00:44:08,920 --> 00:44:10,600
and question and doubt

763
00:44:10,600 --> 00:44:12,260
with tears in our eyes.

764
00:44:12,260 --> 00:44:14,020
All right.

765
00:44:14,020 --> 00:44:17,000
We can see that redemption play out.

766
00:44:17,000 --> 00:44:17,740
Spoiler alert.

767
00:44:17,740 --> 00:44:19,160
We're gonna read the end of the book of Ruth.

768
00:44:19,620 --> 00:44:20,840
And then for the rest of the series,

769
00:44:20,840 --> 00:44:22,280
we'll come back and work our way through.

770
00:44:22,280 --> 00:44:22,700
Sound good?

771
00:44:22,700 --> 00:44:24,000
All right.

772
00:44:24,000 --> 00:44:24,820
Ruth chapter four.

773
00:44:24,820 --> 00:44:27,620
Verse 16.

774
00:44:27,620 --> 00:44:28,500
So Ruth and Naomi,

775
00:44:28,500 --> 00:44:31,720
you remember after I rambled a lot

776
00:44:31,720 --> 00:44:34,120
